                <pb facs="0861" n="791" sameAs="frus:frus1883/pg791"/>

                <head>No. 505.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Hunter to Mr. Foster.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1883-09-18"><hi rendition="#italic">September</hi> 18,
                        1883</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 74.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to instructions to your legation
                    of the 14th April and the 23d June last, in regard to the unwarrantable charges
                    made by Spanish consuls here as fees, I have to inclose a copy of a letter of
                    Messrs. McGrew &amp; Small, of this city, and of protests, which they send
                    herewith, of Joseph Johnson and Joseph Thompson, masters of American schooner
                    Mira A. Pratt, against export fees exacted of them by A. Bermudez, Spanish
                    consul at Key West, Fla., on her cargoes of cattle shipped from that port to
                    Havana on the 30th of April and 14th of May, 1883, and of the other papers
                    mentioned in their letter.</p>
                <p>Submitting the case for pertinent action,</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>W. HUNTER,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Acting
                        Secretary.</hi></signed></closer>







